      "text": "To solve \\(\\log_3 27\\sqrt{3}\\), we start by expressing 27 and \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) in terms of base 3.\n\nFirst, we recognize that \\(27\\) can be written as a power of 3:\n\\[\n27 = 3^3\n\\]\n\nNext, we express \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) as a power of 3:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3} = 3^{1/2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we combine these expressions:\n\\[\n27\\sqrt{3} = 3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2}\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of exponents, specifically \\(a^m \\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}\\), we combine the exponents:\n\\[\n3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2} = 3^{3 + 1/2} = 3^{6/2 + 1/2} = 3^{7/2}\n\\]\n\nWe now need to find \\(\\log_3 3^{7/2}\\). By the definition of logarithms, \\(\\log_b b^x = x\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_3 3^{7/2} = \\frac{7}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}\n\\]",
